-- title:
--   Unique place centred at a smooth point of a plane curve
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x, y \in F$. Let $f \in K[X_0, X_1]$ (a polynomial in two variables over $K$, indexed by `Fin 2`) satisfy $f(x,y) = 0$ under the evaluation $K[X_0,X_1] \to F$ sending $X_0 \mapsto x$, $X_1 \mapsto y$, and assume that $f$ generates the whole kernel of this evaluation: every $g \in K[X_0,X_1]$ with $g(x,y) = 0$ lies in the ideal $(f)$. Assume further that the subfield of $F$ generated by the image of $K$ together with $x$ and $y$ is all of $F$, i.e. $F = K(x,y)$. Let $a, b \in K$ be such that $f(a,b) = 0$ and the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to the second variable does not vanish at $(a,b)$, i.e. $(\partial f/\partial X_1)(a,b) \neq 0$. Then there is exactly one place $v$ of $F$ over $K$ — that is, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring — such that both $x - a$ and $y - b$ are non-units of that valuation subring, i.e. lie in its maximal ideal.
--
--   This is the statement that the local ring of a plane affine curve at a smooth $K$-rational point is a discrete valuation ring, phrased as the existence and uniqueness of a place of the function field $F = K(x,y)$ centred at the point $(a,b)$. It is used to enumerate the places of a specific curve and in the computation of degrees attached to such places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_existsUnique_sub_algebraMap_mem_nonunits_of_eval_pderiv_ne_zero.lean

